    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting way to put it, but can we get a bit more technical with the Amadori rearrangement? I need to focus on the actual molecular pathways. Also, you mentioned things getting \"hyperactive\" in alkaline environments—is there a specific chemical reason why a higher pH accelerates that nucleophilic attack you mentioned?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to dig deeper into the molecular pathways; the Amadori rearrangement is the critical pivot point where the initial, fleeting Schiff base transforms into a more stable intermediate that can actually survive the heat. In the acidic or neutral environment, the amino group of the amino acid attacks the carbonyl carbon of the reducing sugar, forming a Schiff base (an imine). This is a very unstable species, but it quickly undergoes a 1,2-proton shift to become the Amadori rearrangement product (a glycosylamine). This rearrangement is essentially a molecular tectonic shift, moving the double bond from the nitrogen to the oxygen, which stabilizes the structure enough to proceed to the next stage of degradation.\n\nHowever, when you increase the pH, you are essentially turning the dial on the reactivity of the amino group. At higher pH levels, the environment is less acidic, meaning there are fewer protons ($H^+$) available to stabilize the negative charge that develops on the nitrogen during the attack. Without that protonation, the amino group remains a stronger nucleophile, making it much more eager and \"hyperactive\" to attack the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the sugar",
      "source": "target",
